                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "To begin with, there are a lot of comparisons to Final Fantasy Tactics (FFT).  These are warranted.  However, many reviews I read saying 'this ain't it' boils down to grognard behavior who complain that it isn't the same game, like Dragon Age where they go online to complain about every game not being exactly like the first one. \n\nThe writing is okay.  It's not award winning, nor is it painful to read.  The soundtrack is serviceable with some good tracks here and there.  What this does like FFT is classes and strategy.  And where it succeeds more is that characters gain exp in classes at a rate that is actually serviceable without needing excessive grind or guides.  I also love how everything is clearly explained.  It tells you what stats will go up if you level up in this class.  This also allows you to build some seriously good/weird builds that if you approached form a different class, would be completely different.  If I build a dragoon in FFT, the characters will roughly be the same every time.  There are also a TON of classes, even more with the content they put out after release.\n\nI grew up playing FFT on the PS1.  I loved that game.  I didn't get a good spiritual successor until I played this game.  I went back to FFT.  FFT was brilliantly written and pushed the limits of it's system.  But it was slow, some mechanics were not clearly explained, and ultimately, there were much fewer ways to build characters that would be fun to have.  Here, I have so much variety to choose from and it -feels- way less punishing than FFT, and it runs so much faster.  You can like both games.  I do.  I think if you want to scratch that itch to have an army of customized, weird class combos of your imagination; you'd be hard pressed to find a better title.",
